      "content": "In the high-stakes world of database management, ensuring data integrity while allowing multiple users to interact with the same system simultaneously is akin to managing a busy intersection. Without strict rules, chaos ensues. This is where **transaction isolation**, **race conditions**, and **deadlock prevention** come into play. Let\u2019s break these down with clear examples.\n\n### 1. Transaction Isolation\n\n**Transaction isolation** is a property of database transactions that ensures concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other. It defines how and when changes made by one transaction become visible to others. The SQL standard defines four isolation levels, each offering a different balance between performance and data consistency:\n\n- **Read Uncommitted**: The lowest level. Transactions can see uncommitted changes from others. Fast but risky.\n- **Read Committed**: The default in many databases (e.g., PostgreSQL, Oracle). Transactions only see committed data. Prevents \"dirty reads.\"\n- **Repeatable Read**: Ensures that if you read a row once, it won\u2019t change during your transaction. Prevents \"non-repeatable reads.\"\n- **Serializable**: The highest level. Transactions are executed as if they were sequential. Prevents all anomalies but can reduce concurrency.\n\n**Example:**\nImagine",
