      "content": "In the realm of database management systems, ensuring data integrity amidst concurrent access is akin to managing traffic in a bustling city. Without strict rules, chaos ensues. This is where **transaction isolation**, **race conditions**, and **deadlock prevention** come into play. Let\u2019s explore these concepts through a practical scenario: a bank system handling money transfers between two accounts, Account A and Account B.\n\n### 1. Transaction Isolation\n\n**Transaction isolation** defines how visible the changes made by one transaction are to other concurrent transactions. The SQL standard defines four isolation levels, each offering a different balance between performance and data consistency:\n\n- **Read Uncommitted**: Transactions can see uncommitted changes from others. This is the fastest but least safe level, prone to \"dirty reads.\"\n- **Read Committed**: Transactions only see committed data. This prevents dirty reads but allows \"non-repeatable reads\" (where a value changes between two reads in the same transaction).\n- **Repeatable Read**: Ensures that if a transaction reads a value, it will see the same value throughout its duration, even if other transactions modify it. This prevents non-repeatable reads but may allow \"phantom reads\" (where new rows appear in a range query).\n- **",
